WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.753 --> 00:00:05.920
DAVID SEIDMAN: Hi, everyone.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.050
My name is David Seidman.

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:09.330
I'm a product manager
in Google Cloud.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.670
And today we'll be
doing a deep dive

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.220
on data protection
capabilities in compute engine

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:17.270
and persistent disk.

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:20.290
So what we'll be covering today
will be a general overview

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:22.460
of this area.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:25.330
And then some depth on what we
do around backup and disaster

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:26.370
recovery.

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:27.970
A new capability
we're introducing

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:29.830
called disk cloning.

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:31.990
Capabilities for
high availability.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:33.072
Workload consistency.

00:00:33.072 --> 00:00:34.780
And then a set of best
practices that you

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:39.290
can apply to your workloads.

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:44.560
So this is a 300 level session.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:47.830
There'll be some technical
depth on some existing features.

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:52.780
Some new features that
we are announcing.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:56.050
And again, in the context
of best practices,

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:58.010
how to choose the
right tool for the job

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:03.880
so that particular feature or
particular even feature flag

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:06.550
is the right choice for
whatever your scenario is.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:14.990
So in terms of our
mission and who we serve,

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:17.760
our goal is to serve the
most demanding workloads.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.050
So workloads with demanding
recovery point objectives,

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:24.620
recovery time objectives,
workloads that demand

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:30.380
flexibility, whether it's around
cost, or data sovereignty,

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:31.730
or performance.

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:35.780
And the capabilities that
we'll be talking about today

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:39.170
are a foundation for
critical services,

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:41.717
both inside of Google,
and also across

00:01:41.717 --> 00:01:42.800
many different industries.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.092
You can see here some examples.

00:01:44.092 --> 00:01:46.730
Energy, retail, media, finance.

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:47.490
The list goes on.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:50.850
So this is a foundation
that we bet on,

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:52.970
and then also
customers use across

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:54.398
many different industries.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:01.370
So as a high-level overview,
starting with scenarios.

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:06.320
Kind of think about the world
in terms of some major functions

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:08.660
or scenarios.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:12.810
For backup and for
disaster recovery,

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:15.450
we have a highly reliable
high-performance snapshots.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:18.590
And we'll be talking
more about those.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:21.680
If the goal is flexibility
for data mobility, copy data

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.210
management, we'll talk a lot
about what we mean by that.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:27.170
But for example, very quickly
getting data from production

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:28.160
to staging.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:30.170
Or very quickly moving
data from a production

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:33.560
workload to kind of
a scratch environment

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:37.340
where you can do
auditing or verification.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:39.940
Disk clones are the
recommended feature for that.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:42.200
For high availability,
we have a product

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:45.890
that's been GA since
early 2019 called

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:47.550
regional persistent disks.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.498
We'll talk a lot about
how we think about HA.

00:02:50.498 --> 00:02:52.040
And then for
application consistency,

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.480
we have two new features.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:56.510
Google Machine Images and
guest-flush. guest-flush

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:58.737
is actually an existing feature.

00:02:58.737 --> 00:03:00.320
But we've made some
enhancements to it

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:03.850
that we'll be we'll
be talking about.

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:04.350
OK.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:07.290
So some more depth on snapshots.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:11.250
So snapshots provide
resilient backup

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.980
for failures that could
be within the region

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:15.140
or within the service.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:16.950
So what we mean
by that is, if you

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:20.940
have persistent disk
running in a certain region,

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:25.020
a snapshot allows you
to extract that data,

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:28.225
and the snapshot is
created in another region.

00:03:28.225 --> 00:03:30.600
And it happens to be stored
actually in different service

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:34.620
from persistent
disk, stored in GCS.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:37.710
So that means even if there's
a failure, or some sort

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:41.940
of outage, or accidental
issue with your desk,

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:43.650
or with that region,
or even with PD

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.963
overall, the data
is highly protected.

00:03:45.963 --> 00:03:47.380
And there's a lot
of options here.

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:51.720
So for example, by being able
to put a snapshot in any region

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.720
or multi-region, you
can optimize for cost.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.550
You can optimize for resilience.

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.970
For example, putting your
data on a different continent.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:03.600
You can optimize for performance
by keeping your snapshot close

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:06.428
to the disk in the
same region, optimizing

00:04:06.428 --> 00:04:08.220
for how long it takes
to create and restore

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:10.540
that snapshot, or
data sovereignty.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:13.920
So if you need to keep
your data in Montreal

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.987
or in a certain
jurisdiction in Europe,

00:04:17.987 --> 00:04:19.529
you can choose
exactly which location

00:04:19.529 --> 00:04:20.612
the snapshot is stored in.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:27.263
I talked a little about
some of the agility.

00:04:27.263 --> 00:04:29.680
If you have a disk, and you
wish to take a snapshot of it,

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:33.040
that snapshot will go to
any region or multi-region

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:34.360
you select.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:37.690
And if you have a snapshot
in a region or multi-region,

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:39.490
and you wish to
restore it to a disk,

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:43.630
that can directly be used to
create a disk in any region.

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:48.610
In terms of thinking
about performance

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:51.010
for these workflows,
it's important to know

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:53.980
that once you create a
disk from a snapshot,

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:57.780
those disks will run at full
performance, full speed,

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:00.940
as soon as the snapshot
restore completes.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:03.448
So whatever I/O,
throughput, latency

00:05:03.448 --> 00:05:04.990
that you need for
your critical loads

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:08.710
will be there once the disk
is available and attached.

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:10.190
So you don't need
to worry about,

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:12.163
for example, having an
attached disk, which

00:05:12.163 --> 00:05:14.080
sometimes is performing,
and sometimes may not

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:16.430
be performing well.

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:19.360
And in general, as
a basic principle,

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:22.840
the more data that you're moving
to create or restore snapshots,

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:24.102
the longer it will take.

00:05:24.102 --> 00:05:25.810
If you have a very
large disk that you're

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:28.340
taking a snapshot of
for the first time

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:29.800
or that you're
restoring, that will

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:32.080
take longer than an
incremental snapshot

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:35.795
that you're capturing more
frequently with just small data

00:05:35.795 --> 00:05:36.295
changes.

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:42.320
So let's talk a little
about consistency.

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:46.700
So a new area here that
we'll be talking about,

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:49.100
something that's coming
soon, and something that's

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:52.940
now in beta, we want to
ensure that in addition

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:57.200
to the crash consistency
that snapshots provide,

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:00.350
if you need some additional
level of consistency,

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:02.780
either across disks
or up to the level

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:05.750
of the guest for your
application workload,

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:07.320
there's options for that.

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:12.050
So the guest-flush flag has
been available for a long time

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:14.030
for snapshots but
for Windows only,

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:17.120
integrating with Windows VSS.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:19.610
What we're introducing
coming soon

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:23.690
is support for this flag on
Linux and the way it works

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:26.090
is it integrates
with script hooks.

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:30.600
So within the guest that has
the disk attached that you're

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:34.850
snapshotting, you
can create or place

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:38.300
a script that executes
before the snapshot is taken

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:40.520
and after the snapshot is taken.

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:43.610
So that can provide a lot of
flexibility, a lot of control

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:44.630
inside the guest.

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:47.300
So for example, you can
quiesce an application

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:50.240
before the snapshot is taken.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:53.120
And then you can un-quiesce it
after the snapshot is taken.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:54.830
You can trigger automation.

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:58.550
You can run scripting to figure
out what kind of applications

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:02.180
are running on the guest so
that you have visibility for,

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:06.560
let's say, any cataloging
or additional verification

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:10.550
you want to do for
those workloads.

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:14.210
Exactly what those scripts
do and how this flag works

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:16.820
for those script hooks on
Linux depends on the behavior

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:18.600
of the script themselves.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:21.560
So the consistency
or other behavior

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:24.510
is entirely up to those scripts.

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:26.390
And this can be provided
either by something

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:29.120
that you roll your
own, or you can

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:33.650
work with your favorite backup
or disaster recovery provider

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:36.280
that we partner with
for those scripts.

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:41.770
Another feature in
the same category

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:43.270
is Google Machine Image.

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:44.770
This is now in beta.

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:49.810
So this is available
for anyone to use.

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:52.120
Google Machine Image,
sometimes called GMIs,

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:55.720
provide a point-in-time
image of a full instance.

00:07:55.720 --> 00:08:00.160
So that means VM configuration
and data from multiple disks.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:02.620
So if you're running
a workload on a VM,

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:06.220
and the data on multiple disks
that are attached has some sort

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:10.385
of dependency on one another--
let's say data as well

00:08:10.385 --> 00:08:12.760
as the transaction logs for
that data that should be kept

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:14.680
in sync--

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:16.720
when the Google Machine
Image is captured,

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:19.210
it's captured consistently
and anatomically.

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:21.820
So this is great for
instance replication,

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:27.510
backup, restore, or even putting
a GMI into the marketplace.

00:08:27.510 --> 00:08:32.380
Again, it captures the disk
data as well as the metadata

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:33.442
consistently.

00:08:33.442 --> 00:08:34.900
And you can use
this in conjunction

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:37.105
with guest-flush so
that you get consistency

00:08:37.105 --> 00:08:41.049
both across the disks on a VM
as well as up into the workload.

00:08:44.193 --> 00:08:46.610
Let's talk about a new feature
that we touched on a little

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:49.520
in the introduction.

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:51.370
We are now providing
a capability

00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:53.010
we call disk cloning.

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:56.510
So the reason that we've
introduced this capability

00:08:56.510 --> 00:09:02.450
is there's often scenarios where
you may have data on a disk--

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:05.750
let's say a running
disk in production--

00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:10.220
and you very quickly want to
create a new disk from the data

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:11.900
on that source disk.

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:15.113
So today, there's a few
ways you can create disks.

00:09:15.113 --> 00:09:16.280
You can create a blank disk.

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:17.930
You can create a
disk from an image.

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:19.550
You can create a
disk from a snapshot.

00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:21.090
This provides an additional way.

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:25.140
So for example, if
before you had a disk,

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:27.770
and you just wanted to create
another disk in the same zone,

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:29.852
you would have to
create a snapshot

00:09:29.852 --> 00:09:31.310
and then restore
the snapshot down.

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:34.100
So you're moving data
basically up and down

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:37.220
through just a
temporary place holder

00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:40.580
snapshot where you may
not in practice actually

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:43.490
need that snapshot to be
to be a durable backup.

00:09:43.490 --> 00:09:47.340
So just moving that data could
be very slow if you're moving,

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:50.180
let's say, tens of
terabytes of data.

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:52.410
That could take
a very long time.

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:54.770
Disk clones are
optimized for performance

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:57.620
and for this exact scenario
where, again, what you

00:09:57.620 --> 00:09:59.675
want is a disk,
and in particular,

00:09:59.675 --> 00:10:02.420
a disk in that same
local zone where

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:04.570
you're running that workload.

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:06.770
And you can expect this
to typically complete

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:09.030
in a minute or less.

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:12.230
So we talked about scenarios of
quickly bringing up a staging

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:15.080
environment from production.

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:18.950
If you are running a
workload or a database

00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:22.610
and want to run some
kind of verification

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:26.000
or do some sort
of export of data,

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:28.520
rather than running
that export and sort

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:31.130
of walking the disk
while it's still attached

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:33.560
and supporting your
production workload,

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:36.620
you can now clone it,
attach it to a second VM,

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:39.080
and then do whatever
verification, or processing,

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:43.730
or exporting you want on a
completely separate cloned

00:10:43.730 --> 00:10:45.850
system.

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:47.850
For these scenarios, this
is also a cost saving.

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:50.330
So if the goal was to
create a second disk anyway,

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:53.420
now you're saving
money on the snapshots

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:55.740
that are no longer needed.

00:10:55.740 --> 00:10:59.840
The cloned disks are in
many ways ordinary disk.

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:03.950
So they're live, attachable,
fully provisioned,

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:05.780
writable disks.

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:07.965
They run at full speed.

00:11:07.965 --> 00:11:09.090
There are some constraints.

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:13.820
So I mentioned disk clone must
be in the same local zone.

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:16.432
And it also needs to
be of the same type.

00:11:16.432 --> 00:11:17.390
So the same disks used.

00:11:17.390 --> 00:11:19.610
So if you have an SSD
disk, you can clone it

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:23.450
into an SSD disk,
not an HDD disk.

00:11:23.450 --> 00:11:25.410
And this is also designed
for high frequency.

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:27.733
So you can clone a
disk every 30 seconds.

00:11:27.733 --> 00:11:29.900
And again, you can expect
the actual cloning process

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:33.030
to be high performance.

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:36.100
This is just a visual
of one of those flows.

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:38.750
So again, if you're taking
those one-off snapshots

00:11:38.750 --> 00:11:43.190
today for a high value disk
just to create additional disks,

00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:45.230
again, for dev
test, export, audit,

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:50.560
now those one-off snapshots
are not necessary.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:52.580
And another scenario--

00:11:52.580 --> 00:11:55.930
If you are, let's say,
scheduling downtime

00:11:55.930 --> 00:11:58.600
for a workload in
order to do an upgrade,

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:00.520
now you can have
much more protection

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:01.550
with higher performance.

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:03.820
So the scenario here would be--

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:06.970
take a clone of a disk,
save it as a rollback point.

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:11.750
So if you're worried
about the upgrade,

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:14.890
let's say, being unsuccessful
or corrupting the application

00:12:14.890 --> 00:12:20.200
in some way, and you really want
that safe intact rollback point

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:24.110
of before you tried to
change your application,

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:26.590
you would clone it,
stop the workload,

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:29.410
do whatever upgrade
is needed, verify,

00:12:29.410 --> 00:12:32.230
and if the upgrade succeeds,
you can discard that clone.

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:33.377
You don't need it anymore.

00:12:33.377 --> 00:12:35.710
But if it fails and you want
to get your upgrade running

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:37.793
and you want to get your
application running again

00:12:37.793 --> 00:12:40.000
as soon as possible,
just swap in clone disk

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:42.060
and restart your
workload, and you're back

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:43.910
to that healthy state.

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:48.010
So again, this optimizes
this flow where otherwise you

00:12:48.010 --> 00:12:50.620
would need to copy the data
up and down to a snapshot

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:56.860
to get back to that
saved pre-upgrade state.

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:00.720
OK.

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:03.480
So let's switch to
high availability.

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:08.132
So regional persistent
disk is our offering here.

00:13:08.132 --> 00:13:11.620
It provides some basics on
what it is and how it works.

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:15.600
And then we'll talk
about how it supports

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:18.180
different kinds of
failure scenarios

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:20.670
to provide high availability.

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:23.280
So regional persistent
disk provides

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:28.060
synchronous replication between
two zones in the same region.

00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:34.470
So what that means is that as a
workload is writing to a disk,

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:39.870
there's a second replica of
that disk in another zone.

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:43.600
And we ensure that that data
is committed on both sides.

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:46.420
So what that means is that if
you have a single failure--

00:13:46.420 --> 00:13:49.680
let's say you have a network
disconnect or a sustained

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:51.440
outage in zone--

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:57.000
your recovery point objective
can be met at 0 data loss.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:58.470
Versus, let's say,
using snapshots

00:13:58.470 --> 00:14:01.710
where you may take
a snapshot, then

00:14:01.710 --> 00:14:05.478
if your workload is destroyed
or has some sort of outage,

00:14:05.478 --> 00:14:07.020
you can always
restore your snapshot.

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:08.520
But the amount of
data loss that you

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:10.260
would have with the
snapshot scenario

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:11.927
would be the amount
of time that elapsed

00:14:11.927 --> 00:14:14.740
between when you
took the snapshot

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:16.470
and when the outage occurred.

00:14:16.470 --> 00:14:20.640
Here, using regional persistent
disk, your RPO is zero.

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:23.500
This is designed for
maximum availability.

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:28.830
So what that means is, let's say
that your failover zone becomes

00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:29.920
unavailable.

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:31.500
We still maintain
the availability

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:33.880
of the workload in
that primary zone.

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:36.480
So we favor maximum
availability,

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:39.450
which also means that if you
have an outage in one zone,

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:42.592
let's say in your failover
zone, your data is

00:14:42.592 --> 00:14:47.290
still running and intact with no
data loss in your primary zone,

00:14:47.290 --> 00:14:49.530
you want to take
some mitigations.

00:14:49.530 --> 00:14:54.480
We keep your workload running
even when one of those zones

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:55.452
goes down.

00:14:55.452 --> 00:14:56.910
The other thing I
want to emphasize

00:14:56.910 --> 00:15:01.770
is this offering provides
additional replication

00:15:01.770 --> 00:15:04.740
between two zones.

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:08.430
But every zonal persistent
disks meet or exceed

00:15:08.430 --> 00:15:13.620
three copies within each zone
for additional durability.

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:15.870
So just a little more detail
on the failure management

00:15:15.870 --> 00:15:17.070
that I was outlining.

00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:20.290
So you can imagine
two scenarios.

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:24.856
One is if you have a failure
in your primary HA zone,

00:15:24.856 --> 00:15:29.850
we recommend using health
checks to determine if back-ends

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:31.270
are responding to traffic.

00:15:31.270 --> 00:15:35.400
So that's the way you would
detect if there's a failure.

00:15:35.400 --> 00:15:37.260
If there's a failure
in your primary zone,

00:15:37.260 --> 00:15:39.750
and you want a failover
in the secondary zone,

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:41.760
the recovery time
needed to attach

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:47.770
that disk to a failover VM will
typically be a minute or less.

00:15:47.770 --> 00:15:54.030
So again, the data is replicated
to the secondary zone.

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:59.370
And you'll be attaching
a VM to that disk.

00:15:59.370 --> 00:16:01.710
It's actually one
logical disk that's

00:16:01.710 --> 00:16:04.110
represented in those two zones.

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:06.270
The actual RTO that
you achieve will depend

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:07.410
on how you manage your VM.

00:16:07.410 --> 00:16:11.280
So for example, if you have
a hot standby VM that's

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:14.250
running all the time versus
a VM that you need to start,

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:18.860
that, of course, will affect
your recovery time objective.

00:16:18.860 --> 00:16:22.200
In the scenario where a failure
occurs in the secondary HA

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:25.950
zone, if you're thinking about
a primary secondary pair,

00:16:25.950 --> 00:16:29.310
then again, the data that
you're continuing to maintain

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:33.660
and your available
primary zone is there.

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:35.670
But you've lost
your HA protection

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:38.010
because your secondary
zone is now down.

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:39.840
So in that case,
the best practice,

00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:42.300
and in fact, we would always
recommend this best practice,

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:43.950
is to use scheduled snapshots.

00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:46.770
So scheduled
snapshots can ensure

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:50.560
that you're at least bounding
your recovery point objective,

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:54.360
even if you have that secondary
zone become unavailable

00:16:54.360 --> 00:16:56.590
so that if there is
then a second failure--

00:16:56.590 --> 00:16:59.820
let's say there's a regional
outage or some other sort

00:16:59.820 --> 00:17:01.560
of loss--

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:05.833
you now have snapshots as an
additional layer of protection.

00:17:05.833 --> 00:17:07.500
The other thing that's
important to know

00:17:07.500 --> 00:17:11.130
about regional persistent
disk is catch up

00:17:11.130 --> 00:17:13.569
and failure recovery
is automatic.

00:17:13.569 --> 00:17:16.920
So let's say there's a
network outage in replicating

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:18.300
to a second zone.

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:20.010
Once that's resolved,
the replicas

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:22.500
will automatically be caught
up and brought in sync.

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:25.530
OK.

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:29.550
So let's talk about some best
practices for these features.

00:17:29.550 --> 00:17:32.760
So the first point we made
in the context of clones.

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:37.820
Avoid temporary snapshots
whenever possible.

00:17:37.820 --> 00:17:40.680
So again, we know
that for scenarios

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:44.010
where the goal is to create
a new disk immediately

00:17:44.010 --> 00:17:47.790
for the scenarios that
we have described,

00:17:47.790 --> 00:17:51.870
copying back and forth to a
snapshot is a waste of time,

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:55.110
and also a waste of resources,
and an unnecessary cost.

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:58.200
So you can use clones for
much higher performance

00:17:58.200 --> 00:18:00.810
and cost saving benefits.

00:18:00.810 --> 00:18:05.600
The second best practice is if
you want to very quickly create

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:08.240
new disks, especially
across region,

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:11.250
images are the best
resource for that.

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:14.120
So again, if you have
a single data source,

00:18:14.120 --> 00:18:18.050
and you want to very quickly
create large numbers of disks,

00:18:18.050 --> 00:18:21.920
including between
regions, images

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:23.550
are optimized for that scenario.

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:26.290
So there's local
caching that we do

00:18:26.290 --> 00:18:28.730
in persistent disk
in any target zone

00:18:28.730 --> 00:18:31.810
that you're starting
to create new disks,

00:18:31.810 --> 00:18:35.957
and you'll see much faster
high performance disk creation.

00:18:35.957 --> 00:18:37.790
The third best practice
that we talked about

00:18:37.790 --> 00:18:40.130
in the context of
regional persistent disk,

00:18:40.130 --> 00:18:43.130
but applies really
everywhere, is

00:18:43.130 --> 00:18:47.100
to schedule hourly snapshots for
backup and disaster recovery.

00:18:47.100 --> 00:18:52.240
So we have a built-in
scheduled snapshots capability.

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:55.370
This is a great way to
limit any risk of data loss.

00:18:55.370 --> 00:19:03.450
If there is, again, some outage,
or disaster, or human error.

00:19:03.450 --> 00:19:07.250
The other thing to
consider is some customers

00:19:07.250 --> 00:19:10.610
like to take daily
snapshots, which is great.

00:19:10.610 --> 00:19:14.720
If that frequency can be
increased so that snapshots

00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:17.840
are taken every 6, hours or
12 hours, or something daily,

00:19:17.840 --> 00:19:20.000
that will lead to
smaller uploads

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:22.910
rather than uploading all of
the changes for the last day.

00:19:22.910 --> 00:19:26.540
And that will support faster
snapshot creation times.

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:29.900
OK.

00:19:29.900 --> 00:19:33.740
So hopefully this will be
useful as sort of a reference

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:37.620
chart in thinking about
scenarios, features,

00:19:37.620 --> 00:19:40.740
and what they're
purpose-built for.

00:19:40.740 --> 00:19:42.230
So again, snapshots
we really think

00:19:42.230 --> 00:19:46.430
of as optimized for
long-term data backup.

00:19:46.430 --> 00:19:50.510
You can find pricing
information on our website.

00:19:50.510 --> 00:19:52.610
And, again, information
about exactly how

00:19:52.610 --> 00:19:55.460
we manage full
snapshots and then

00:19:55.460 --> 00:19:58.610
incremental changes of
those snapshots as you

00:19:58.610 --> 00:20:01.220
take more snapshots
from the same disk.

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:03.680
And again, if what you need
is geo-redundancy or long-term

00:20:03.680 --> 00:20:06.500
backup, it's the best option.

00:20:06.500 --> 00:20:08.300
If the goal is
high availability,

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:12.810
regional persistent disk
is purpose-built for that.

00:20:12.810 --> 00:20:15.590
It's not built for backup.

00:20:15.590 --> 00:20:18.560
So if a region is somehow
completely unavailable

00:20:18.560 --> 00:20:20.990
or destroyed in
some way, you would

00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:23.360
need snapshots in
addition to regional PD

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:26.270
to have that durable
geo-redundant backup.

00:20:26.270 --> 00:20:28.400
But regional persistent
disk gives you

00:20:28.400 --> 00:20:35.060
that zero RPO for a single
failure and that very low RTO.

00:20:35.060 --> 00:20:38.210
Disk clones also not intended
for disaster recovery.

00:20:38.210 --> 00:20:40.250
Those are optimized
for efficiently

00:20:40.250 --> 00:20:46.130
creating new disks in the
same zone from a production

00:20:46.130 --> 00:20:46.760
workload.

00:20:46.760 --> 00:20:49.910
So again, we talked about the
examples of export offloading,

00:20:49.910 --> 00:20:54.380
forking data into staging
very frequently optimized

00:20:54.380 --> 00:20:55.388
for that purpose.

00:20:55.388 --> 00:20:56.930
And then as we look
at Google Machine

00:20:56.930 --> 00:20:59.930
Images and the
guest-flush option,

00:20:59.930 --> 00:21:01.590
those are optimized
for consistency.

00:21:01.590 --> 00:21:05.060
So whether you need consistency
across multiple disks that

00:21:05.060 --> 00:21:07.790
are working together to
support a workload on one VM,

00:21:07.790 --> 00:21:12.290
or whether or not
you want consistency

00:21:12.290 --> 00:21:16.370
up into the guest itself, again,
for application quiescence,

00:21:16.370 --> 00:21:21.800
or verification, or additional
information and control that's

00:21:21.800 --> 00:21:24.110
only available
inside of the guest.

00:21:27.020 --> 00:21:29.100
And looking at it from
the scenario perspective,

00:21:29.100 --> 00:21:31.440
just to recap--

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:35.280
backup and DR, we
recommend using snapshots.

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:39.330
For efficiently moving
data within a zone

00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:42.815
to bring up new workloads,
we recommend disk clones.

00:21:42.815 --> 00:21:45.713
And then for high availability,
regional persistent disks.

00:21:45.713 --> 00:21:47.130
And then we have
these two options

00:21:47.130 --> 00:21:50.750
depending on the nature of
the application consistency

00:21:50.750 --> 00:21:54.410
that you need.

00:21:54.410 --> 00:21:56.160
So thank you very much.

00:21:56.160 --> 00:22:02.020
And we look forward to
hearing your feedback.

